To determine the appropriate mixing model for this binary surfactant system, we examine how the measured mixed critical micelle concentration (\text{CMC}_{\text{mix}}) compares to the theoretical ideal behavior predicted by **Clint's equation** (Ideal Solution Theory):

\frac{1}{\text{CMC}_{\text{ideal}}} = \frac{\alpha_1}{\text{CMC}_1} + \frac{\alpha_2}{\text{CMC}_2}

For instance, at a bulk mole fraction \alpha_2 = 0.95 (\alpha_1 = 0.05):
* Ideal \text{CMC}: \left(\frac{0.05}{1.8} + \frac{0.95}{8.3}\right)^{-1} \approx 7.03\text{ mM}
* Measured \text{CMC}_{\text{mix}}: 3.6\text{ mM}

The measured \text{CMC}_{\text{mix}} is significantly lower than the ideal prediction across the compositions, demonstrating strong negative deviations from ideality (synergism in mixed micelle formation). Accounting for this non-ideality and intermolecular interactions between the two different surfactant headgroups/tails requires the introduction of a non-interaction parameter (\beta) via **Regular Solution Theory** (often referred to as the **Rubingh model**).

